"""Practical extensions for schema,type,security and data-change subtopics.

These are assessed with native demonstrations and an explicit rubric,not a
fictional automatically graded reference for every possible migration plan.
"""
LABS={
'Beginner':[
('Valid baseline','Implement the core in a fresh practice copy. Return the requested result and a before/after object or value snapshot. Explain which business rule it establishes.','The baseline satisfies every stated column/type/key rule;the observed result matches the core reference.'),
('Missing input','Propose one missing required input from the core and one explicitly allowed missing input. Execute both where supported,or prove that the second case is not supported. Return accepted/rejected with the exact field or object.','Required and optional are distinguished from metadata;unsupported optionality is rejected,not invented.'),
('Exact boundary','Find a real boundary of this core:representable value,width,nullability,valid identifier or object dependency. Demonstrate the boundary and its nearest invalid neighbor.','The chosen boundary is documented by the actual type/object contract and a native observable result.'),
('Two independent rows','Run two independent valid instances of the core with different keys or values. Return both outcomes and prove changing one cannot overwrite the other. For a scalar construct,use separate values rather than inventing persistent columns.','Different business keys/variables do not share unintended state;all requested outcomes are visible.'),
('Duplicate proposal','Repeat an identical core request. Determine whether it is a legal repeat,a duplicate-key conflict,or a second independent value. Return the observed result without suppressing the rejection.','Behavior follows actual key/object/construct semantics;repeat does not automatically mean idempotency.'),
('Metadata evidence','Return a compact specification of the core\'s actual output/object type,nullability,key membership or scope. Obtain evidence from SQL Server metadata or a native demonstrator,not a guessed type name.','Metadata agrees with the object/value actually created and distinguishes runtime values from object definitions.'),
('Unknown versus zero','Create the smallest core-related example separating missing data,known zero and absent row/object. Return three labelled outcomes;reject a representation unsupported by this construct.','Null,zero and absence are not conflated;no invented persistent representation is claimed for CURSOR/ROWVERSION.'),
('Wrong type input','Supply an input of the wrong declared type and a correctly typed equivalent. Show whether SQL Server rejects,converts,rounds or truncates it,and return the resulting value/error category.','The result is established by native execution;implicit conversion is not assumed lossless.'),
('Dependency order','List the core\'s real prerequisites,then execute one deliberately incorrect order in a practice copy and the correct order. Return the failing dependency and the final valid state.','Only actual PK/FK,scope or object dependencies are asserted;an unrelated earlier lesson is not a prerequisite.'),
('Rejected request preserves state','Choose a core request that must be rejected under a stated rule. Capture the relevant state before and after;prove no prohibited value/object survives.','Rejected state is absent;independent earlier valid work is not falsely reported as undone.'),
('Deterministic presentation','Return the core result with exact column names and a deterministic order where rows exist. Demonstrate a tied/identical-value case using different valid keys.','Final ordering is explicit;native types and required values are retained rather than flattened to misleading text.'),
('Least sufficient permission','Identify the minimum permission needed for the core and demonstrate an allowed and denied action under instructor-provided principals. A local decision table is acceptable until a native principal is available.','The scope of the grant is explicit;permission on a similarly named object does not count.'),
('Name and scope collision','Construct two legal scopes/keys with the same display name or similar identifier. Prove the core resolves the intended one using actual identifiers.','Display labels are not assumed unique;qualification and stable keys determine identity.'),
('Empty state','Execute the core with an empty source or absent target as appropriate. Return a legal empty result,required creation,or precise precondition failure.','Empty is handled according to the construct;no fabricated row or successful mutation is reported.'),
('Independent acceptance check','Implement the core and an independent assertion derived from its business rule. Introduce one wrong value/object definition in a practice copy and show the assertion catches it.','The assertion does not merely rerun the implementation predicate;the seeded defect produces a visible failure.'),
],
'Intermediate':[
('Batch with mixed validity','Prepare five core-related requests:two valid,one missing-required,one conflicting key/object and one representation error. Validate all before applying accepted requests individually;return one disposition per input position.','Every input has one disposition;invalid requests never become default-valued successes;changes persist and instructor resets between attempts.'),
('Lossless round trip','Move a valid core value/object specification through a second declared representation and back. Return full equality evidence and a counterexample where a superficially similar conversion loses information.','The chosen second representation is explicit;byte length alone is not proof of semantic equality.'),
('All conflicting keys','Build a candidate batch containing repeated keys under the real equality/collation rules. Return every member of each conflict group;do not select an arbitrary survivor.','Conflicts are identified with actual comparison semantics,including trailing spaces/case where applicable.'),
('Drift before change','Change one existing type,nullability,key,scope or prerequisite relative to the core contract. Write a preflight that reports the exact difference and prevents an incompatible operation.','Preflight checks actual state and runs before writes;it does not blindly recreate the object.'),
('Application bypass','Implement the core rule at the application/input layer and at the authoritative database/native boundary. Show a direct bypass attempt and its observable rejection or a precise limit of database enforcement.','Every write path is considered;an application-only check is not mistaken for database integrity.'),
('Partial input correction','Load or model three good and two bad core-related inputs. Return a corrected plan only for the bad inputs while preserving the good keys/values. Do not silently change unknown values to zero.','Corrections are explicit and targeted;good state is unchanged and bad state is traceable.'),
('Idempotency decision','Add a durable request identity to a repeatable core operation or an external operation journal where the construct itself is not stateful. Identical repeat returns prior outcome;different payload under the same ID is CONFLICT.','Business identity is distinct from row display name;the journal cannot claim an unexecuted action succeeded.'),
('Reference membership','Add one valid and one missing parent/reference/scope dependency to a core example. Return all missing dependencies before proceeding;do not disable constraints to make the case pass.','Real dependencies are checked in their correct direction;unsupported references are explicitly rejected.'),
('Schema version precondition','Declare schema/contract version1,plan the core change,then change to version2 before execution. Reject the stale plan and regenerate it against version2.','The accepted plan names the version it validated;stale assumptions cannot overwrite a newer definition.'),
('Before-image restoration','Capture enough evidence to reconstruct the original practice state after the core. Demonstrate a separate explicit repair/reset plan,without claiming that an ordinary failed request undoes prior committed changes.','Before images contain keys and exact values/definitions;restore order respects real dependencies.'),
('Representation-aware reconciliation','Compare two core-related datasets/specifications with matching counts but different complete values. Return missing,extra and changed items using stable keys or full-row multiplicity.','A matching count/checksum/grand total is not accepted as a proof of equality.'),
('Safe dynamic identifier','Accept a requested core object identifier only from a declared allowlist. Demonstrate a valid identifier and a crafted separator/quote attack;the rejected input must never become executable SQL.','Identifiers and values are handled separately;raw interpolation is not accepted as parameterization.'),
('Precision and aggregate boundary','Choose a core-related value where individual rows fit but a derived aggregate/combined representation may not. Demonstrate the risk and an explicit safe representation,or prove why this construct cannot be aggregated.','Intermediate and final types are both considered;unsupported operations are rejected instead of coerced.'),
('Two independent implementations','Implement the core business rule with two independent supported strategies. Compare complete outcomes on normal,empty,boundary and invalid cases. Where only one native construct is possible,use an independent validation model for the second.','Both strategies enforce the same contract and expose disagreements as concrete counterexamples.'),
('Auditable decision table','Deliver a finite table of core-related input states and expected accepted/rejected/converted outcomes,then execute it. Include one context/type/dependency interaction that an isolated happy-path check misses.','Expected outcomes are declared before execution;the interaction changes a real decision and is not just another constant.'),
],
'Advanced':[
('Zero-loss representation migration','Design and demonstrate an expand/validate/switch migration of the core\'s representation or object contract with old/new consumers active. Include a nonrepresentable input and prove it is quarantined before switch. Return compatibility and full-value reconciliation evidence.','A lossless representation/explicit rejection policy exists;old consumers are supported until the declared cutover;no silent rounding/truncation/key loss.'),
('Conflicting concurrent actors','Give two sessions or a deterministic native/local interleaving attempting incompatible core-related changes after reading the same old state. Implement a commit-time version/ownership precondition and show exactly one wins.','There is an observable race and a guarded resolution;checking only before computation is insufficient. Do not invent transaction syntax in student prerequisites.'),
('Crash after accepted effect','Model the core operation accepting its database effect but the caller missing the response. Use durable action identity to discover the accepted outcome before retrying. Include a conflicting replay payload.','Accepted effects are not duplicated;unknown remains distinct from failed;replay payload conflict is detected.'),
('Dependency graph rebuild','Rebuild a core-related graph of objects/records with one self-reference and a cross-table dependency. Return a valid staged execution order and an explicit diagnosis for a cycle that cannot be created in one simple pass.','Actual keys/types/scopes are preserved;deferred data loading or staged nullable links are justified without permanently disabling constraints.'),
('Collation and canonicalization trap','Construct distinct source keys/representations that collapse under a proposed normalization,collation or conversion used by the core. Build a pre-migration collision report and a deterministic reject/remap plan with lineage.','All colliding originals are reported;canonicalization is not allowed to silently discard one;where the native type has no collation,use an applicable representation collision instead.'),
('Intermediate overflow or precision loss','Construct values that individually satisfy the core but break an intermediate computation or derived representation. Supply the corrected native expression/schema and an independent exact-value check. For nonnumeric constructs,use a concrete length/encoding/structural-loss boundary.','Casting only the final result is not accepted when overflow/loss occurs earlier;the proof includes the actual problematic intermediate step.'),
('Restartable chunked repair','Repair a large core-related dataset in stable-key chunks with a durable journal. Stop after an accepted chunk and before acknowledgment,then resume without skipping or applying a business correction twice. Return the final reconciliation.','Chunk boundaries and action identities are durable;the plan does not use unstable offset paging over changing data.'),
('Stale authorization at execution','Authorize a core-related object/action,then revoke or change its ownership before execution. Demonstrate a fresh authorization/version check at the actual action boundary and show the old plan cannot leak data or modify the object.','The proof distinguishes missing identity,stale policy and wrong object scope;an earlier allow is not sufficient.'),
('Mixed-version import with tombstones','Model versioned core-related records arriving out of order with deletes. Highest version wins;equal-version different content is CONFLICT. Show a delayed older row cannot resurrect a deleted key and return complete final state.','Tombstone/version metadata is retained;null data is not used as an ambiguous delete marker;conflicts are visible.'),
('Atomic publication boundary','Build a new validated core-related representation in scratch state and publish it through a single versioned current pointer or equivalent native boundary. Inject failure before and after publication;readers must see a complete old or new contract.','Prepared and published states are distinct;readers cannot observe a mix;post-publication response failure is reconciled by version.'),
('Type or scope spoofing','Construct an untrusted input that passes a superficial text/type/name check but targets the wrong object or changes meaning after implicit conversion. Implement a typed,fully qualified validation boundary and prove both the attack and a valid request are classified correctly.','Logical identity and actual native representation are checked;sanitizing display text alone is not accepted.'),
('Nonunique history correction','Model two corrections for the same business key/effective time plus a later correction. Produce a deterministic CONFLICT for contradictory equal-version input,retain prior valid history and show the later input does not erase evidence of the unresolved conflict.','There is no arbitrary TOP1/DISTINCT survivor;history boundaries and conflict lineage remain explicit.'),
('Owner failure and fencing','Give two simulated/native workers performing the core-related migration/import. The first loses its lease but finishes late. Use a monotonically increasing fencing token at publication so its stale result cannot overwrite the new owner.','Lease expiry alone is insufficient;the actual write checks the token and returns a visible stale-owner conflict.'),
('Independent corruption detector','Build an independent scanner for the core invariant across existing rows/objects,including one orphan/missing dependency,one representation mismatch and one duplicate/version conflict where supported. Seed all three and show complete diagnostics even when global counts/totals still match.','The scanner is not a copy of the mutation predicate;all applicable seeded defects are detected and unsupported states are explicitly identified.'),
('Adversarial native case study','Deliver the core plus one multi-step failure case combining three applicable hazards from precision,duplicates,scope,dependency,concurrency,encoding or unknown outcome. Provide native SQL or a precisely bounded local simulator,expected states and a recovery trace.','All three hazards interact in a concrete case;correctness evidence,not vague recommendations or a benchmark request,is required.'),
]}


def extension(level,index):
    title,task,rubric=LABS[level][index]
    return dict(title=title,task=task,rubric=rubric)
